  <!-- EDUCATIONAL SEO & USER GUIDE ARTICLE -->
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <div class="space-y-8">

      <!-- SECTION 1: WHAT IS THE LEAST COMMON MULTIPLE? -->
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          What is the Least Common Multiple (LCM)?
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          In mathematics, the <strong>Least Common Multiple</strong>, also known as the <strong>lowest common multiple (LCM)</strong> or <strong>least common divisor (LCD)</strong> of two or more integers <em>a</em> and <em>b</em>, is the smallest positive integer that is evenly divisible by each number in the set without leaving a remainder. It is commonly denoted as <strong>LCM(a, b)</strong> or <strong>LCM(a, b, c)</strong>.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          For example:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 list-disc list-inside text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 font-mono">
          <li><strong>LCM(2, 3) = 6</strong> (6 is the smallest number divisible by both 2 and 3)</li>
          <li><strong>LCM(6, 10) = 30</strong> (30 is divisible by both 6 and 10; smaller shared multiples do not exist)</li>
          <li><strong>LCM(12, 18, 30) = 180</strong> (180 is the smallest integer divisible by all three numbers)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 2: HOW TO USE THE CALCULATOR -->
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-base sm:text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-2">
          <span>⚙️</span> How to Use the LCM Calculator
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          This calculator finds the LCM of any set of two or more positive integers and generates complete mathematical proofs with steps:
        </p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1.5 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          <li><strong>Enter your numbers:</strong> Type two or more positive integers separated by commas (<code>,</code>) or spaces into the input box (e.g. <code>12, 18, 30</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>Formatting rule:</strong> Do not include commas within individual numbers (for example, enter <code>2500, 1000</code> rather than <code>2,500, 1,000</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>Click Calculate LCM:</strong> The tool instantly displays the final LCM, the GCF, the product, and three interactive step-by-step methods: prime factorization with exponents, iterative GCF formulas, and listing multiples.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 3: THE 6 METHODS FOR FINDING LCM -->
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
            How to Find the Least Common Multiple (LCM): 6 Different Methods
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            There are several viable mathematical methods for calculating the least common multiple. Each method has its own strengths depending on whether you are working by hand, solving algebra problems, or analyzing large numbers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- METHOD 1: LISTING MULTIPLES -->
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 sm:p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/60 space-y-3">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-2">
            <span>1️⃣</span> 1. Listing Multiples (Brute Force Method)
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            The most fundamental approach is the listing method: write out the multiples of each number in ascending order until at least one positive integer appears on every list. The smallest number appearing across all lists is the LCM.
          </p>

          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/70 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40 space-y-2 text-xs">
            <div class="font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">Example A: Find LCM(18, 26)</div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">List positive multiples of each integer:</p>
            <div class="font-mono space-y-1 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
              <div><strong>Multiples of 18:</strong> 18, 36, 54, 72, 90, 108, 126, 144, 162, 180, 198, 216, <span class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">234</span>, 252...</div>
              <div><strong>Multiples of 26:</strong> 26, 52, 78, 104, 130, 156, 182, 208, <span class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">234</span>, 260...</div>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 font-semibold">
              The smallest shared multiple is <strong>234</strong>. Therefore, <strong>LCM(18, 26) = 234</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/70 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40 space-y-2 text-xs">
            <div class="font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">Example B: Find LCM(6, 7, 21)</div>
            <div class="font-mono space-y-1 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
              <div><strong>Multiples of 6:</strong> 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, 36, <span class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">42</span>, 48, 54, 60...</div>
              <div><strong>Multiples of 7:</strong> 7, 14, 21, 28, 35, <span class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">42</span>, 49, 56, 63...</div>
              <div><strong>Multiples of 21:</strong> 21, <span class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">42</span>, 63...</div>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 font-semibold">
              The smallest number on all three lists is <strong>42</strong>. So, <strong>LCM(6, 7, 21) = 42</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>
          <p class="text-[11px] text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 italic">
            Note: While listing multiples is easy to understand, it becomes tedious and impractical for large numbers or sets with little common factorization.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- METHOD 2: PRIME FACTORIZATION & EXPONENTS -->
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 sm:p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/60 space-y-3">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-2">
            <span>2️⃣</span> 2. Prime Factorization Method &amp; Prime Factorization with Exponents
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Prime factorization breaks each number down into its prime building blocks. The LCM is obtained by identifying all unique prime factors and raising each to the <strong>highest power</strong> (largest exponent) with which it appears in any single number's factorization, then multiplying those values together.
          </p>

          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs">
            <!-- Example 21, 14, 38 -->
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/70 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40 space-y-2">
              <div class="font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">Example: LCM(21, 14, 38)</div>
              <ul class="font-mono space-y-0.5 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
                <li>21 = 3 × 7</li>
                <li>14 = 2 × 7</li>
                <li>38 = 2 × 19</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
                Unique prime factors: 2, 3, 7, 19 (each appearing at most once per number).
              </p>
              <div class="font-mono font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400">
                LCM = 2 × 3 × 7 × 19 = 798
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Example 12, 30 -->
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/70 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40 space-y-2">
              <div class="font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">Example: LCM(12, 30)</div>
              <ul class="font-mono space-y-0.5 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
                <li>12 = 2 × 2 × 3 = 2² × 3¹</li>
                <li>30 = 2 × 3 × 5 = 2¹ × 3¹ × 5¹</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
                Highest power of 2 is 2², of 3 is 3¹, of 5 is 5¹.
              </p>
              <div class="font-mono font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400">
                LCM = 2² × 3¹ × 5¹ = 4 × 3 × 5 = 60
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Example 24, 300 -->
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/70 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40 space-y-2">
              <div class="font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">Example: LCM(24, 300)</div>
              <ul class="font-mono space-y-0.5 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
                <li>24 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 = 2³ × 3¹</li>
                <li>300 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 5 × 5 = 2² × 3¹ × 5²</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
                Highest powers: 2³, 3¹, 5².
              </p>
              <div class="font-mono font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400">
                LCM = 2³ × 3¹ × 5² = 8 × 3 × 25 = 600
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Example 12, 18, 30 -->
            <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/70 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40 space-y-2">
              <div class="font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">Example: LCM(12, 18, 30)</div>
              <ul class="font-mono space-y-0.5 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
                <li>12 = 2² × 3¹</li>
                <li>18 = 2¹ × 3²</li>
                <li>30 = 2¹ × 3¹ × 5¹</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
                Highest powers: 2², 3², 5¹.
              </p>
              <div class="font-mono font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400">
                LCM = 2² × 3² × 5¹ = 4 × 9 × 5 = 180
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- METHOD 3: CAKE / LADDER METHOD -->
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 sm:p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/60 space-y-3">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-2">
            <span>3️⃣</span> 3. The Cake Method (Ladder, Box, or Grid Method)
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            The <strong>Cake method</strong> (also referred to as the ladder, box, factor box, or grid method) uses compact division steps. Numbers are written in a horizontal layer. You divide by a prime number that evenly divides into <em>two or more numbers</em> in the layer, bringing down the quotients and any undividable numbers to the next layer. Repeat until no prime number divides into two or more remaining terms.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-2 pt-1">
            <div class="text-xs font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
              Worked Example: Find LCM(10, 12, 15, 75) using the Cake/Ladder Method
            </div>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">
              <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">
                <thead class="border-b border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-800/80 font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="p-2.5">Prime Divisor (Left Column)</th>
                    <th class="p-2.5 text-center">Col 1</th>
                    <th class="p-2.5 text-center">Col 2</th>
                    <th class="p-2.5 text-center">Col 3</th>
                    <th class="p-2.5 text-center">Col 4</th>
                    <th class="p-2.5">Operation Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100 dark:divide-slate-800 font-mono text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
                  {cakeMethodSteps.map((row) => (
                    <tr class="hover:bg-slate-50/60 dark:hover:bg-slate-800/40">
                      <td class="p-2.5 font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400">{row.prime}</td>
                      <td class="p-2.5 text-center">{row.n1}</td>
                      <td class="p-2.5 text-center">{row.n2}</td>
                      <td class="p-2.5 text-center">{row.n3}</td>
                      <td class="p-2.5 text-center">{row.n4}</td>
                      <td class="p-2.5 font-sans text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 text-[11px]">{row.note}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-xl border border-blue-100 bg-blue-50/60 p-3.5 dark:border-blue-900/40 dark:bg-blue-950/30 text-xs space-y-1">
            <div class="font-bold text-blue-900 dark:text-blue-200">L-Shape Product Rule:</div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
              The LCM is the product of all numbers forming the "L" shape (the left-hand column prime divisors multiplied by the bottom row remaining values, ignoring 1s):
            </p>
            <div class="font-mono font-bold text-blue-800 dark:text-blue-300">
              LCM = 2 × 3 × 5 × (1 × 2 × 1 × 5) = 30 × 10 = 300
            </div>
            <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">
              Therefore, <strong>LCM(10, 12, 15, 75) = 300</strong>.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- METHOD 4: DIVISION METHOD -->
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 sm:p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/60 space-y-3">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-2">
            <span>4️⃣</span> 4. The Division Method
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            In the <strong>Division method</strong>, you arrange the numbers in a row and divide by the smallest prime number that divides evenly into <em>at least one</em> of the numbers. Bring down quotients for divisible numbers and carry down untouched numbers. Continue dividing until the final row consists entirely of 1s. The product of the prime numbers in the first column is the LCM.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-2 pt-1">
            <div class="text-xs font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
              Worked Example: Find LCM(10, 18, 25) using the Division Method
            </div>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800">
              <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">
                <thead class="border-b border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-800/80 font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="p-2.5">Prime Divisor (Column 1)</th>
                    <th class="p-2.5 text-center">10</th>
                    <th class="p-2.5 text-center">18</th>
                    <th class="p-2.5 text-center">25</th>
                    <th class="p-2.5">Step Description</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100 dark:divide-slate-800 font-mono text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
                  {divisionMethodSteps.map((row) => (
                    <tr class="hover:bg-slate-50/60 dark:hover:bg-slate-800/40">
                      <td class="p-2.5 font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400">{row.prime}</td>
                      <td class="p-2.5 text-center">{row.n1}</td>
                      <td class="p-2.5 text-center">{row.n2}</td>
                      <td class="p-2.5 text-center">{row.n3}</td>
                      <td class="p-2.5 font-sans text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 text-[11px]">{row.note}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-xl border border-blue-100 bg-blue-50/60 p-3.5 dark:border-blue-900/40 dark:bg-blue-950/30 text-xs space-y-1">
            <div class="font-bold text-blue-900 dark:text-blue-200">Column Multiplication:</div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
              Multiply all the prime numbers in the first column:
            </p>
            <div class="font-mono font-bold text-blue-800 dark:text-blue-300">
              LCM = 2 × 3 × 3 × 5 × 5 = 450
            </div>
            <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">
              Therefore, <strong>LCM(10, 18, 25) = 450</strong>.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- METHOD 5: GREATEST COMMON FACTOR (GCF) FORMULA -->
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 sm:p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/60 space-y-3">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-2">
            <span>5️⃣</span> 5. Finding LCM using the Greatest Common Factor (GCF / GCD)
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            A factor (or divisor) is an integer that divides evenly into another number. The <strong>Greatest Common Factor (GCF)</strong>, also widely known as the <strong>Greatest Common Divisor (GCD)</strong>, Highest Common Factor (HCF), Highest Common Divisor (HCD), Greatest Common Measure (GCM), or Highest Common Measure (HCM), is the largest positive integer that divides all numbers in the set.
          </p>

          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/70 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40 space-y-2 text-xs">
            <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">The Two-Number GCF-LCM Formula:</div>
            <div class="font-mono text-sm font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 p-2.5 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 text-center">
              LCM(a, b) = (a × b) / GCF(a, b)
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Example: Find LCM(6, 10)</strong>
            </p>
            <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-1 font-mono text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
              <li>Find GCF(6, 10) = 2</li>
              <li>Calculate: (6 × 10) / 2 = 60 / 2 = <strong>30</strong></li>
              <li>So LCM(6, 10) = <strong>30</strong></li>
            </ol>
          </div>

          <div class="rounded-xl border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50/60 p-3.5 dark:border-amber-900/40 dark:bg-amber-950/30 text-xs space-y-1.5">
            <div class="font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200">
              Important: Extension to Three or More Numbers (Iterative Application)
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              The direct equation <code>LCM(a, b) × GCF(a, b) = a × b</code> holds strictly for <strong>two numbers</strong>. For three or more numbers (e.g. <em>a</em>, <em>b</em>, <em>c</em>), you determine the LCM by computing iteratively:
            </p>
            <div class="font-mono font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-300 text-center bg-white/70 dark:bg-slate-900/60 p-2 rounded-lg border border-amber-200/60 dark:border-amber-900/50">
              LCM(a, b, c) = LCM(LCM(a, b), c)
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Example: Find LCM(21, 14, 38)</strong>
            </p>
            <ul class="font-mono space-y-1 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
              <li>Step 1: GCF(14, 38) = 2 ⟹ LCM(14, 38) = (14 × 38) / 2 = 532 / 2 = <strong>266</strong></li>
              <li>Step 2: GCF(266, 21) = 7 ⟹ LCM(266, 21) = (266 × 21) / 7 = 5,586 / 7 = <strong>798</strong></li>
              <li>Therefore, <strong>LCM(21, 14, 38) = 798</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- METHOD 6: VENN DIAGRAM METHOD -->
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 sm:p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/60 space-y-3">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-2">
            <span>6️⃣</span> 6. The Venn Diagram Method
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Venn diagrams use overlapping circles to visualize prime factor distributions. For two numbers <em>A</em> and <em>B</em>, each number is represented by a circle:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1.5 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            <li><strong>Intersection (Center Overlap):</strong> Contains the prime factors shared by both numbers. Multiplying the numbers in this overlap gives the <strong>Greatest Common Factor (GCF)</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Outer Lobes:</strong> Contain the unique prime factors belonging to only that individual number.</li>
            <li><strong>Union (All Regions Combined):</strong> To find the <strong>LCM</strong>, multiply every prime factor across all sections of the Venn diagram (counting shared factors in the intersection only once).</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 4: LCM OF DECIMAL NUMBERS -->
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          How to Find the LCM of Decimal Numbers
        </h2>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          While the classical definition of the least common multiple applies to positive integers, decimal quantities with terminating fractional parts can be evaluated using a standard decimal scaling technique:
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/70 p-4 sm:p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/50 space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Identify decimal places (D):</strong> Count the maximum number of decimal places across all numbers in your set. Let that count be <em>D</em>.</li>
            <li><strong>Convert to integers:</strong> Shift the decimal point <em>D</em> places to the right (multiply each number by 10<sup>D</sup>) to convert every value into an integer.</li>
            <li><strong>Calculate integer LCM:</strong> Find the LCM of this new set of integers using prime factorization or the GCF formula.</li>
            <li><strong>Re-scale result:</strong> Divide the resulting integer LCM by 10<sup>D</sup> (shift the decimal point <em>D</em> places back to the left). This yields the least common multiple for the original decimal set.</li>
          </ol>

          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 space-y-1 text-xs">
            <div class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Example: Find the LCM of 0.12 and 0.18</div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
              Both numbers have <em>D = 2</em> decimal places. Multiply by 10² = 100 to obtain integers 12 and 18.
            </p>
            <div class="font-mono text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
              LCM(12, 18) = 36 ⟹ 36 / 100 = <strong>0.36</strong>
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 text-[11px]">
              0.36 is the smallest positive decimal evenly divisible by 0.12 (0.36 / 0.12 = 3) and 0.18 (0.36 / 0.18 = 2).
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 5: MATHEMATICAL PROPERTIES OF LCM -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          Mathematical Properties of the Least Common Multiple
        </h2>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          The least common multiple obeys several fundamental algebraic laws:
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs">
          <!-- Commutative -->
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 space-y-1.5">
            <div class="font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 uppercase text-[11px] tracking-wider">
              Commutative Property
            </div>
            <div class="font-mono font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white text-sm">
              LCM(a, b) = LCM(b, a)
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              The order of the numbers does not affect the least common multiple. For example, LCM(4, 6) = LCM(6, 4) = 12.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Associative -->
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 space-y-1.5">
            <div class="font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 uppercase text-[11px] tracking-wider">
              Associative Property
            </div>
            <div class="font-mono font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white text-sm">
              LCM(a, b, c) = LCM(LCM(a, b), c)
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              When calculating the LCM of three or more numbers, grouping order does not matter: LCM(a, LCM(b, c)) produces the identical result.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Distributive -->
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 space-y-1.5">
            <div class="font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 uppercase text-[11px] tracking-wider">
              Distributive Property
            </div>
            <div class="font-mono font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white text-sm">
              LCM(da, db, dc) = d × LCM(a, b, c)
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Multiplying each term by a positive integer <em>d</em> scales the least common multiple by exactly that factor <em>d</em>.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- GCF Relationship -->
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 space-y-1.5">
            <div class="font-bold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400 uppercase text-[11px] tracking-wider">
              GCF-LCM Relationship (Pairs)
            </div>
            <div class="font-mono font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white text-sm">
              LCM(a, b) × GCF(a, b) = a × b
            </div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              The product of two positive integers is always equal to the product of their LCM and GCF. Hence, GCF(a, b) = (a × b) / LCM(a, b).
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- SECTION 6: COMPARISON TABLE OF METHODS -->
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-base sm:text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-2">
          <span>📊</span> Comparison: Which LCM Method Should You Use?
        </h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/60">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">
            <thead class="border-b border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-800/90 text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200 font-bold">
              <tr>
                <th class="p-3">Calculation Method</th>
                <th class="p-3">Best Suited For</th>
                <th class="p-3">Computation Speed</th>
                <th class="p-3">Key Practical Advantage</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100 dark:divide-slate-800/60 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
              {methodsComparison.map((row) => (
                <tr class="hover:bg-slate-50/70 dark:hover:bg-slate-800/40">
                  <td class="p-3 font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">{row.name}</td>
                  <td class="p-3">{row.bestFor}</td>
                  <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-400">{row.speed}</td>
                  <td class="p-3">{row.keyAdvantage}</td>
