      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 sm:p-6 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900/60 space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-base sm:text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-2">
          <span>❓</span> Frequently Asked Questions About LCM
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40">
            <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200 hover:text-blue-600 dark:hover:text-blue-400">
              What is the difference between LCM and GCF (GCD)?
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              The <strong>Greatest Common Factor (GCF)</strong> is the largest number that divides <em>into</em> all the given numbers, making it smaller than or equal to the smallest number. The <strong>Least Common Multiple (LCM)</strong> is the smallest number that all the given numbers divide into, making it greater than or equal to the largest number in the set.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40">
            <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200 hover:text-blue-600 dark:hover:text-blue-400">
              Can the LCM be smaller than any of the input numbers?
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              No. By definition, a multiple of a positive integer <em>n</em> must be at least <em>n</em>. Therefore, the least common multiple of a set of numbers is always greater than or equal to the maximum number in that set: <code>LCM(a, b, ...) ≥ max(a, b, ...)</code>.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40">
            <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200 hover:text-blue-600 dark:hover:text-blue-400">
              What is the LCM of two coprime or prime numbers?
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              If two numbers are prime or coprime (meaning they share no common prime factors, so GCF = 1), their least common multiple is simply their direct product: <code>LCM(a, b) = a × b</code>. For example, LCM(5, 7) = 35 and LCM(8, 9) = 72.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50/50 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950/40">
            <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200 hover:text-blue-600 dark:hover:text-blue-400">
              How is LCM used in fractions as the Least Common Denominator (LCD)?
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              The <strong>Least Common Denominator (LCD)</strong> of two or more fractions is exactly the least common multiple of their denominators. Finding the LCM allows you to convert unequal fractions to equivalent fractions with a shared denominator for straightforward addition and subtraction.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>
